      "problem": "Solve for $n$ where ${16}^{2n}={8}^{n+1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $16={2}^{4}$ and $8={2}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({2}^{4})}^{2n}={({2}^{3})}^{n+1}\\,\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{8n} & ={2}^{3(n+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8n & =3(n+1) \\\\ 8n & =3n+3 \\\\ 5n & =3 \\\\ n & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
